# coder-fork @VERSION@ – install

Coder @CODER_VERSION@ (the fork's open-source build) for an air-gapped Kubernetes cluster: with the public images the
chart runs in your registry, push this folder's images, then one `helm install` with one values file. The chart
`coder-airgapped-@VERSION@.tgz` installs the whole platform, and on a bare cluster also its prerequisites (Traefik with
the Gateway API, the CloudNativePG operator).

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `coder-airgapped-@VERSION@.tgz` | The chart |
| `<name>_<tag>.tar.gz` | Our @IMAGE_COUNT@ images, `docker save` + gzip (@IMAGES_SIZE@); one over 1900 MiB is split into `<file>.part-00`, `-01`, ... (`cat` joins them) |
| `push-images.sh` | Checks the files, then loads each image and pushes it to your registry, where the chart pulls it (docker only) |
| `PUBLIC-IMAGES.txt` | The public images the chart runs: their path in your registry and their tested digests (step 1) |
| `pull-public-images.sh` | Optional, on a connected machine: saves those public images into this folder, so `push-images.sh` pushes them too |
| `IMAGES.txt` | Each image: its reference, file(s), size, config and manifest digests |
| `values.example.yaml` | The values to start `values.yaml` from: every input of the install |
| `values-ha.example.yaml` | High availability, on top of your values |
| `SHA256SUMS` | Every file here (`sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS`); `SHA256SUMS.joined`: the split archives, joined |
| `CVE-REPORT.md` | The CVE scan of the release's images (ours): no fixable finding |

## Before you start

- **Kubernetes 1.27 or newer with amd64 (x86_64) nodes** – the images are amd64 only.
- **A container registry** the nodes pull from (ECR, Harbor, a plain registry ...) – `$REGISTRY` below, the
  chart's `global.registry`. Log in to it from where you run `push-images.sh` (`docker login $REGISTRY`; ECR:
  `aws ecr get-login-password | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin $REGISTRY`).
- **The public images the chart runs, in that registry** (step 1): they are not in this folder - `pull-public-images.sh`
  saves them into it on a connected machine. They come from their own projects, through your site's usual import and
  scanning; their CVEs are those projects' to fix.
- **A domain** for the platform: Coder at `coder.<domain>`, workspace apps at `*.<domain>`. Your users' machines must
  resolve both to the Gateway (Traefik's Service). A domain of one label (`vm`, `lab`) needs
  `global.wildcardHost: "*.coder.<domain>"`: browsers refuse a wildcard certificate and cookies directly under it.
- **Optional:** your own CA and wildcard certificate (else the chart makes them), Keycloak (OIDC) for your users'
  sign-in (else the first owner signs in with a password).
- **Storage** for persistent volumes (a default StorageClass). A provisioner that pulls a helper image when it makes
  the first volume - minikube's and kind's local-path pull `docker.io/busybox` - must find it on every node: pull it
  there before the cluster goes offline, or no volume is ever made.

Tools: `docker`, `helm`, `kubectl`.

## 1. Images

The public images the chart runs (with high availability and its own prerequisites too) must be in your registry at
these paths - each at the digest tested, or a later build of the same tag:

| In your registry | The public image (tested digest) |
|---|---|
@PUBLIC_IMAGES@

(The path is the image's own without its registry: `docker.io/traefik:v3.7.12` → `$REGISTRY/traefik:v3.7.12`.)
Your registry has them already? Then `./push-images.sh --ours-only $REGISTRY` below. Otherwise save them into this
folder on a connected machine and carry the folder across - `push-images.sh` then pushes them with ours:

```bash
./pull-public-images.sh        # on a connected machine, in this folder: public/, IMAGES-public.txt, SHA256SUMS.public
```
 Where the cluster already runs Traefik or CloudNativePG (`global.bundled` off, step 2), their
images are not needed.

Then push:

```bash
./push-images.sh $REGISTRY     # --dry-run first if you like; ECR=1 creates missing ECR repositories
```

It checks every file against `SHA256SUMS` (and `SHA256SUMS.public`), then loads, tags and pushes each image to
`$REGISTRY/<its path without the source registry>` (e.g. `ghcr.io/user187x/coder-platform:T` →
`$REGISTRY/user187x/coder-platform:T`) - where the chart pulls it - and ends with a summary.

## 2. Values

```bash
cp values.example.yaml values.yaml    # change every CHANGE-ME: global.domain, global.registry ($REGISTRY)
```

Every input of the install is in this one file:

- **The prerequisites** (`global.bundled`): the chart installs @TRAEFIK@ with the Gateway API v1.6.0 CRDs and
  CloudNativePG @CNPG@. Set either to `false` where the cluster already runs it - a second CloudNativePG operator
  would fight the first one over the database; with your own Traefik, also set `global.gateway` to your Gateway.
- **Certificates:** `secrets.generateTls: true` makes a CA and a certificate for the domain, Coder's host and the
  wildcard host (once; upgrades keep them). Your users' browsers must trust that CA:
  `kubectl -n <namespace> get secret tls-ca -o jsonpath='{.data.ca\.crt}' | base64 -d >ca.crt`. Or give yours:
  `generateTls: false`, `secrets.tlsCa.create: true` with `caCrt`, `secrets.wildcardTls.create: true` with
  `tlsCrt`/`tlsKey` (PEM, inline with `|`).
- **How users reach it:** `traefik.service.type: LoadBalancer` (a cloud's) or `NodePort` (bare metal, minikube,
  kind: `https://coder.<domain>:<the websecure NodePort>`).
- **In-cluster DNS:** pods (workspace agents, Coder's own tailnet) reach Coder by its URL. Where the cluster's DNS
  does not resolve your domain (minikube, kind, bare metal), set `global.inClusterDNS.enabled: true`: the chart adds
  rewrite rules for Coder's host and the wildcard host to the cluster's CoreDNS (and takes them out on uninstall).
- **Kubelets with self-signed certificates** (minikube, kind, kubeadm's defaults): `metricsServer.kubeletInsecureTLS:
  true`, else metrics-server cannot read them.
- **Keycloak (OIDC):** `global.oidc.issuerUrl`, `secrets.oidcClient` (create + the client secret) and, when another CA
  signed Keycloak's certificate, `secrets.oidcCa`. An issuer Coder cannot reach leaves password sign-in only.
  Coder resolves the issuer's host from inside the cluster: your site's DNS must answer it there
  (`global.inClusterDNS` covers only Coder's own hosts; on minikube or kind, add the issuer to CoreDNS's `hosts`).
- **High availability:** add `-f values-ha.example.yaml` after `values.yaml` (one Coder serves, a standby takes over;
  the database's synchronous standby and pooler; Traefik on two replicas).

## 3. Install

```bash
helm install coder-fork coder-airgapped-@VERSION@.tgz -n coder-fork --create-namespace -f values.yaml --timeout 20m
```

Any release name and namespace. `helm install` returns once the platform is up: the database created as soon as the
CloudNativePG operator answers, then Coder, its first owner and the workspace templates - a few minutes (watch
`kubectl -n coder-fork get pods`). `--timeout 20m` gives a slow cluster (storage, image pulls) the room: past Helm's
default 5 minutes the release is marked failed while the platform goes on coming up - then run the same command as
`helm upgrade --install`, which completes it.

## 4. Sign in

Open `https://coder.<domain>` and sign in as the first owner: `global.firstUser.email` with its password (yours, or
the random one: `kubectl -n coder-fork get secret coder-init-auth -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d`) -
then change it (your avatar › Account › Security).

The next release: push its images, then `helm upgrade coder-fork coder-airgapped-<next>.tgz -n coder-fork -f
values.yaml`. (CRDs: Helm installs the bundled prerequisites' CRDs once and never upgrades them; a release that needs
newer ones says so.)

## 5. Uninstall

```bash
helm uninstall coder-fork -n coder-fork --wait    # first: its hooks stop the workspaces that mount a Cloud Drive,
                                                  # remove the database and the CoreDNS rules
kubectl delete namespace coder-fork               # then the rest (the volumes, Coder's workspaces)
```

The Cloud Drives' volumes stay (their data: a reinstall adopts each account's again). One of an account the next
install does not have - a former first owner, say - stays `Released`: `kubectl get pv | grep coder-safe-`, and delete
those you no longer need.

In this order. Uninstalling first lets a hook stop every workspace that mounts a Cloud Drive while the Cloud Drive's
CSI driver can still unmount it. Deleting the namespace straight away removes the driver too, and those workspace
pods never finish terminating. If that happened: `kubectl -n coder-fork delete pod <pod> --grace-period=0 --force`,
and on its node `umount -l` the pod's leftover `fuse` mounts.
